\subsection{Token Importance Spatial Distribution}
\label{app:token_heatmap}

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_token_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{$\pi_0.5$ token importance spatial distribution (16$\times$16 grid, single observation). Left: L2 norm heatmap reveals a center-high, edge-low pattern (center rows 7--9: norm $\sim$265--267; edge rows 0, 14--15: norm $\sim$235--236). Right: at $k{=}0.9$, L2 norm selection (green) retains center tokens while discarding 50\% of removed tokens from the outer ring; random selection (red marks) distributes deletions uniformly.}
\label{fig:token_heatmap}
\end{figure}

Token importance heatmaps reveal that $\pi_0.5$'s 768 vision tokens (reshaped to a 16$\times$16 spatial grid after the $\sqrt{d}$-scaled linear projection) exhibit a center-high, edge-low L2 norm pattern. At $k{=}0.9$ (26 tokens removed), L2 norm-based selection discards predominantly edge tokens: 50\% of removed tokens fall in the outermost ring despite edge positions comprising only 23\% of the grid. This spatial concentration is consistent with the center tokens encoding the robot arm and target object, while edge tokens capture less task-relevant background.

Random removal at the same ratio samples deletions uniformly across the spatial grid, creating a combinatorial probability of simultaneously removing multiple high-importance center tokens. As more tokens are removed ($k{=}0.925 \to 0.85$), this probability increases sharply, consistent with the observed cliff onset. The asymmetry explains why L2 norm selection maintains a near-baseline plateau through the cliff zone: it preferentially preserves the high-importance center region that random removal disrupts by chance.

