\subsection{Hypergeometric Cliff Model}
\label{app:cliff_model}

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_cliff_model.pdf}
\caption{Hypergeometric cliff model for $\pi_0.5$ Object random pruning. (a)~Model-predicted collapse probability (dashed) vs.\ observed success rate (solid) across the cliff zone $k \in [0.85, 0.95]$; RMSE${=}$0.025. (b)~$P(\text{collapse})$ curves for Object ($C{=}91$, concentrated) and Spatial ($C \approx 5$, distributed), illustrating why Object exhibits a sharp phase transition absent on Spatial.}
\label{fig:cliff_model}
\end{figure}

The random cliff on $\pi_0.5$ Object (Section~\ref{sec:cross_suite}) can be modeled as a hypergeometric sampling problem. Among $N{=}729$ vision tokens, $C{=}91$ are critical---defined as tokens whose L2 norm exceeds the 87.5th percentile, corresponding to the center-high region identified in the importance heatmap (Appendix~\ref{app:token_heatmap}). When $\lfloor N(1{-}k) \rfloor$ tokens are removed uniformly at random, the number of critical tokens removed follows a hypergeometric distribution $\mathrm{Hypergeometric}(N, C, \lfloor N(1{-}k) \rfloor)$. We define performance collapse as removing $\geq T{=}6$ critical tokens simultaneously; fitting $T$ to minimize RMSE against observed success rates in the cliff zone yields $T{=}6$ with RMSE${=}$0.025.

The model predicts three characteristic retention ratios: $k{=}0.963$ where $P(\text{collapse}){=}10\%$ (cliff onset), $k{=}0.938$ where $P(\text{collapse}){=}50\%$ (midpoint), and $k{=}0.903$ where $P(\text{collapse}){=}90\%$ (near-complete collapse). The cliff width $\Delta k \approx 0.06$ between 10\% and 90\% collapse probability matches the observed transition from 69.4\% SR ($k{=}0.95$) to 12.0\% SR ($k{=}0.90$). The model captures the cliff onset and sharpness but does not explain the V-shape recovery at $k \leq 0.7$, where success rate rebounds to 77.2\%; characterizing the recovery mechanism is left to future work.

The cross-suite difference emerges naturally from the critical token count. Object manipulation concentrates critical information in $C{=}91$ tokens (12.5\% of the grid), creating a steep increase in $P(\text{collapse} \geq T)$ over a narrow $k$ range. Spatial tasks distribute critical information across approximately $C \approx 5$ tokens, making the probability of simultaneously removing $\geq T$ critical tokens negligible at all tested retention ratios---consistent with Spatial's graceful degradation (58.4\% at $k{=}0.85$ vs.\ Object's 5.0\%).

